   "html_text": "Until the second half of the 20th century, certain characteristics of laws of nature were taken for granted by philosophers of science: Laws of nature were taken to be true, logically contingent, universal statements that support counterfactual claims. Laws were taken to play a major role in explanation, induction, confirmation, causation, and in counterfactual reasoning. The paradigm cases for such laws were taken from (fundamental) physics. Newton’s second law ⟦M⟧ or the Schrödinger-equation f",
